ctive but it takes money which we  don't have so I was going to mention  actually the Israel situation because I  think it's sort of interesting you know  in America and in most of the world we  spend so much time talking about the  israeli-palestinian conflict and the  wider conflict with the Arab world and  to me it seems that it's the only reason  we talk about it so much and we're  talking about the most ridiculously tiny  piece of land on earth is because it's  simple to frame the argument it's Jews  verse Arabs or Jews verse Muslims  however you you want to frame it and I  think the story of the Kurds because  it's confuse Muslim are you know it  makes it much more confusing much less  people don't understand the sunni-shia  divide so that it really has a lot to do  with just defining what the actual  problem is in a sort of simple way  that's true and I think one very  worrying factor that's we're seeing now  in the United States but also in Europe  is this fear of the Muslim the fear that  every Muslim is out to kill us you know  and fortunately that is not true I know  that might be the perception and I'm not  denying that there are Muslims who want  to kill Americans or Europeans and Isis  is has a distorted version of Islam that  it wants to propagate but the vast  majority of Kurds are Muslim but I would  say when moderate we believe in  coexistence is part of our culture we  look to the west and we admire the west  we admire the progress the technology  the advances in science and health  putting a man on the moon hopefully a  woman on the moon soon a so on and we  admire that and we want part of it it  doesn't mean that we're going to let go  of our culture and our heritage and you  know become I don't know palm beach or  something Kurdistan will be Kurdistan  and we'll have its culture but why not  share what we have with you and you with  us and we can both benefit from it yeah  so in a weird way it almost sounds to me  like the society you're what you're  trying to build in some ways is so  reverse of what goes on in that part of  the world it's hard for people to wrap  their brains around that this could  happen it almost would exacerbate it  would show people Wow Iraq really did  fail cuz look what's happening in Iraq  but meanwhile somehow the Kurds did it  so it really flips everything on its  head that you know that this flower  could happen in the middle of the desert  I think Kurdistan is the success story  in Iraq and it needs to be nurtured I  think your description of it as a flower  is apt I think it is a flower but like  every flower it needs to be tended it  needs to be nurtured it can easily be  knocked down and this is really where we  are today I should say though Kurdistan  is an island of stability and peaceful  coexistence  but there are other places in the Middle  East - I don't want to make out let's  just go to Sun if you look at Lebanon of  course they have problems I don't want  to but Lebanon has Arabs Druze  Christians Muslims you know there are  other places around the Middle East it's  just that they're not allowed to thrive  and this is where the United States can  really play a role nurture these places  that do look to the West that do want to  share values of human rights and liberty  and want to do business for the United  States for example but they need  nurturing because we are surrounded by  hostile attitudes yeah you know it's  funny I think actually in a way you're  proving my point because we don't talk  about Lebanon either which yeah  there are Christians and Muslims in  Lebanon that are that are getting along  so every time something's good happening  in the Middle East for some reason we  seem unable to talk about it  but let's talk a little bit about your  family history because you come from a  long line of people in your family that  have helped the Kurdish movement for a  long time and and you've paid the price  personally so can you tell me a little  bit about that sure I guess a good  starting point is my grandfather my  maternal grandfather my family is  originally from Sinjar which most people  know as a Yazidi town but actually in  the old days it used to be mixed Muslims  Christians Yazidis Turkmen and others  and my grandfather was very prominent  played a very prominent role in Sinjar  advocating for people's rights and so on  and he and many many of my relatives  including male female and children  relatives have been imprisoned exiled  tortured various things basically  because they stood out stood up for  Kurdish rights then my father and mother  both of them also played a very  important role in the Kurdish liberation  movement my father was actually in the  leadership of one of the political  parties and he was a Peshmerga in his  young days carrying arms and fighting  the Iraqi regime at the time then he  evolved into a really a statesman and a  diplomat my mother was a housewife and  the mother  those are needed - those are needs in  society dismisses people like that but  she was also a campaigner I mean she  really campaigned for Kurdistan and for  people's rights and my father at one  time was kind of prime minister in exile  of of a Kurdistan Regional Government in  exile this was in the 1970s and then in  the late 90s he became the deputy prime  minister of the Kurdistan region and at  the time that he was killed in 2004 he  was deputy prime minister so you said  that my family had  pay the price we have but I don't want  to exaggerate my family situation  because many many Kurdish people have  suffered enormously and my family is  just like this my in February 2004 my  elder brother and my father were killed  in a suicide bombing by Islamist  extremists along with many other people  and of course it had a huge impact on my  family on me personally and so today in  my work I'm very proud of the legacy  that I've inherited and I feel that I  have a very high standard to live up to  so I have to make sure I don't let down  what they built up and that I continue  to serve my people the way they did and  the way they wanted me to  you
 

